  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cusco">
  <data key="d0">Cusco</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Cusco is a region in Peru with a significant Quechua-speaking population, where the Quechua language is considered a course of mandatory instruction.&lt;SEP&gt;Cusco is a city in Peru known for its cultural and historical significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Cusco is a department within the political organization of Peru.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1b2c9e7decea4bad65244951a79c736&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbbadd51840d1a9868ce0bad28760899</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783974228</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ministry of Culture">
  <data key="d0">Ministry of Culture</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Ministry of Culture is the governmental body in Peru tasked with formulating policies to promote intercultural dialogue, guarantee indigenous rights, and develop tools for intercultural competency among public servants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Indigenous Peoples">
  <data key="d0">Indigenous Peoples</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Indigenous Peoples are a collective group with unique languages, cultures, and rights, for whom the Ministry of Culture aims to ensure access to justice and protect their linguistic and cultural identity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Servants">
  <data key="d0">Public Servants</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Public Servants are government officials and employees who are expected to have basic knowledge of the Quechua language to better serve the Quechua-speaking population.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intercultural Policies">
  <data key="d0">Intercultural Policies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intercultural Policies refer to the set of principles and actions aimed at promoting cultural diversity, mutual respect, and the inclusion of indigenous languages and cultures within public institutions and services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Cusco Collao">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Cusco Collao</data>
  <data key="d1">language</data>
  <data key="d2">Quechua Cusco Collao is a specific dialect of the Quechua language spoken in the Cusco region of Peru.&lt;SEP&gt;Quechua Cusco Collao is a language with specific terms for parental relationships that vary based on the gender of the person being referenced.&lt;SEP&gt;Quechua Cusco Collao is a language variant with distinct terms for familial relationships based on gender.&lt;SEP&gt;Quechua Cusco Collao is a dialect of the Quechua language.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14a99f648724916d3a3cdaf7f55100f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2885b89726947656d89803f886c108ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b876ea97897fb4dacbb9fe3b4411869e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783974135</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intercultural Competency">
  <data key="d0">Intercultural Competency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intercultural Competency is the ability of public servants to effectively communicate and interact with citizens of different cultural backgrounds, especially those with languages other than Spanish.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Access to Justice">
  <data key="d0">Access to Justice</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Access to Justice is a fundamental right that ensures individuals can seek legal redress and have their rights protected through the legal system, regardless of their linguistic or cultural background.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Language Rights">
  <data key="d0">Language Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Language Rights are the rights of individuals and communities to use, maintain, and develop their languages, and to have access to services in their native languages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intercultural Dialogue">
  <data key="d0">Intercultural Dialogue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intercultural Dialogue is a process of open and respectful communication between individuals or groups with different cultural backgrounds, aimed at fostering understanding, tolerance, and mutual respect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intercultural Manuals">
  <data key="d0">Intercultural Manuals</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Intercultural Manuals are bilingual guides designed to facilitate communication between public servants and indigenous citizens, promoting mutual understanding and respect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bilingual Education">
  <data key="d0">Bilingual Education</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bilingual Education is an educational approach that incorporates the teaching of two languages, in this case, Quechua and Spanish, to ensure cultural preservation and effective communication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783967829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua-Spanish Bilingualism">
  <data key="d0">Quechua-Spanish Bilingualism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Quechua-Spanish Bilingualism is the ability to communicate effectively in both Quechua and Spanish, which is crucial for public servants to understand and serve the Quechua-speaking population.&lt;SEP&gt;The concept of bilingualism in Quechua and Spanish is emphasized, especially in official and public service settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-903eae28c3678cd1170d8d47a488993c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b090bec40f9538cbb2debd0666e4bf32</data>
