<node id="Food">
  <data key="d0">Food</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Food is a multifaceted concept, encompassing various aspects of sustenance and survival. It is a legal term that refers to the essential needs of life, including food, clothing, housing, health, education, and recreation. This term highlights the fundamental requirements for human well-being and development.

From a biological perspective, food is any substance that living organisms consume to obtain nourishment and sustain life. It is a basic necessity, providing the body with the energy and nutrients required for growth, development, and maintenance. This definition emphasizes the edible nature of food and its role in supporting physiological functions.

In the context of child support and family law, food takes on a specific legal meaning. It is considered a basic necessity that parents or caregivers are legally obligated to provide for their children. This includes not only the physical sustenance but also other essential expenses related to the children's upbringing and overall well-being. The cost of food is a significant factor in calculating the financial support required for a child's healthy development.

The term 'food' thus bridges the gap between legal and biological realms, recognizing its critical role in human survival, development, and the legal responsibilities of caregivers. It is a fundamental component of life, ensuring the health and well-being of individuals and their dependents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b08a136ff0b17a4cbed6181955b9e88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73fa586f1c761d07237556ddd2413a79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-197d939d7a066d3322d1fe67add2fe4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9ec333aebf1f09df2b4159280bbd3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c59dfd0a7722cdd48deb0ca97fc9d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0316609c53848e02f3ae95df7bf25b68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0afa2cdd77fb81053ae8e35465c22abe</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783976806</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Legal Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal obligation refers to the duty to provide food, as mandated by a court resolution, which is essential for the well-being of family members.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal obligation is a duty or responsibility imposed by law.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal Obligation is a duty imposed by law.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal obligation is a duty or responsibility imposed by law, such as the obligation to provide food to a spouse, partner, or child.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal Obligation is a duty or responsibility imposed by law.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal obligation is a duty or responsibility imposed by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b08a136ff0b17a4cbed6181955b9e88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f97dc4fc3d1d9806a3e490289388951&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-197d939d7a066d3322d1fe67add2fe4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c59dfd0a7722cdd48deb0ca97fc9d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f2e0660bbd2b6136970934b1b421679</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783976865</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Omision">
  <data key="d0">Omision</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Omision is a legal concept referring to the failure to act or perform a required action, which can lead to legal consequences.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b08a136ff0b17a4cbed6181955b9e88</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783968036</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Doctrine">
  <data key="d0">Legal Doctrine</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal doctrine provides a distinction between two types of omissions: omision propia (own omission) and omision impropia (improper omission).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b08a136ff0b17a4cbed6181955b9e88</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783968036</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 149 of the Penal Code">
  <data key="d0">Article 149 of the Penal Code</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 149 of the Penal Code addresses the crime of omision a la asistencia familiar (omission of family assistance), outlining the penalties for non-compliance with court-ordered food payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b08a136ff0b17a4cbed6181955b9e88</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783968036</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First Paragraph of Article 149">
  <data key="d0">First Paragraph of Article 149</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The first paragraph of Article 149 specifies that failure to comply with a court-ordered food obligation is punishable by up to three years in prison or community service.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b08a136ff0b17a4cbed6181955b9e88</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783968036</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second Paragraph of Article 149">
  <data key="d0">Second Paragraph of Article 149</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The second paragraph of Article 149 discusses the penalties for agents who simulate another food obligation, maliciously renounce or abandon their work, with a minimum of one and a maximum of four years in prison.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b08a136ff0b17a4cbed6181955b9e88</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783968036</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Court Resolution">
  <data key="d0">Court Resolution</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A court resolution is a legal document issued by a court that establishes the obligation to provide food and serves as the basis for legal action in case of non-compliance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b08a136ff0b17a4cbed6181955b9e88</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783968036</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
