<node id="Tenencia Definitiva">
  <data key="d0">Tenencia Definitiva</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenencia Definitiva is a type of custody established by a judicial sentence or a conciliation agreement, requiring a new legal act for modification.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenencia Definitiva is a type of custody established by a court judgment or a conciliation agreement, requiring a new agreement or court ruling for modification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7c7f4b075e7719e5980150cd54d9c5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c41555f6b1e63737c4c742bcd62c6783</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783972993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tenencia Provisional">
  <data key="d0">Tenencia Provisional</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenencia Provisional is a precautionary measure granted during a legal process to ensure the immediate well-being of a minor before the final sentence.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenencia Provisional is a temporary custody measure to ensure the well-being of children or adolescents before a final decision, granted in a judicial process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7c7f4b075e7719e5980150cd54d9c5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c41555f6b1e63737c4c742bcd62c6783</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783972993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tenencia Conjunta">
  <data key="d0">Tenencia Conjunta</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenencia Conjunta refers to a situation where both parents live with their children and jointly exercise their rights and duties.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenencia Conjunta refers to a custody arrangement where both parents live with their children, jointly exercising parental rights and responsibilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7c7f4b075e7719e5980150cd54d9c5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c41555f6b1e63737c4c742bcd62c6783</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783972993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tenencia Compartida">
  <data key="d0">Tenencia Compartida</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenencia Compartida is a legal concept originating from Anglo-Saxon law, allowing children to live with both parents even after separation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenencia Compartida is a custody arrangement where parents share custody, contrasting with situations where only one parent has custody.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7c7f4b075e7719e5980150cd54d9c5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c41555f6b1e63737c4c742bcd62c6783</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783972993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Art. 81 Codigo de los Ninos y Adolescentes">
  <data key="d0">Art. 81 Codigo de los Ninos y Adolescentes</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Art. 81 of the Codigo de los Ninos y Adolescentes establishes the priority of Tenencia Compartida, except in exceptional cases where Tenencia Exclusiva is granted, prioritizing the best interest of the child.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7c7f4b075e7719e5980150cd54d9c5a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783971426</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Art. 88 Codigo de los Ninos y Adolescentes">
  <data key="d0">Art. 88 Codigo de los Ninos y Adolescentes</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Art. 88 of the Codigo de los Ninos y Adolescentes conditions the right of the non-custodial parent to visit their child on the payment of child support.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7c7f4b075e7719e5980150cd54d9c5a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783971426</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
