<node id="Cónyuge">
  <data key="d0">Cónyuge</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A cónyuge is a spouse, referring to either a husband or a wife in a marital relationship.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d80cc2eeef6ca2518edabac4f237f40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ascendente">
  <data key="d0">Ascendente</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An ascendente is an ancestor or relative from whom one is descended, such as a parent or grandparent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d80cc2eeef6ca2518edabac4f237f40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Descendiente">
  <data key="d0">Descendiente</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A descendiente is a descendant or relative who is descended from a particular ancestor, such as a child or grandchild.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d80cc2eeef6ca2518edabac4f237f40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artículo 474">
  <data key="d0">Artículo 474</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Artículo 474 is a specific article within the Código Civil, establishing reciprocal obligations for providing sustenance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d80cc2eeef6ca2518edabac4f237f40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artículo 288">
  <data key="d0">Artículo 288</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Artículo 288 is another article in the Código Civil, emphasizing the duty of assistance and fidelity between spouses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d80cc2eeef6ca2518edabac4f237f40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artículo 6">
  <data key="d0">Artículo 6</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Artículo 6 is a constitutional article that underscores the equal rights and duties of children and the responsibility of parents to provide for them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d80cc2eeef6ca2518edabac4f237f40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grandchildren">
  <data key="d0">Grandchildren</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Grandchildren are discussed as potentially receiving support from their grandparents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9e969af4c3467565b30ab2cc8e1f611&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grandparents">
  <data key="d0">Grandparents</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Grandparents may be required to provide alimony to their grandchildren.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Descendants">
  <data key="d0">Descendants</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Descendants, including children and grandchildren, are responsible for providing alimony to their ancestors.&lt;SEP&gt;Descendants are mentioned as potential recipients of alimentary obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b6abaffe898ea6968fedc45e3bdcab4</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783975307</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solidarity Family Principle">
  <data key="d0">Solidarity Family Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Solidarity Family Principle is a legal concept that forms the basis for alimony obligations among family members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ancestors">
  <data key="d0">Ancestors</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ancestors, including parents and grandparents, are entitled to receive alimony from their descendants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brothers">
  <data key="d0">Brothers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Brothers are obligated to provide alimony to each other, regardless of their specific relationship.&lt;SEP&gt;Brothers are mentioned as potential recipients of alimentary obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b6abaffe898ea6968fedc45e3bdcab4</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783975307</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CNA Article 93">
  <data key="d0">CNA Article 93</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">CNA Article 93 outlines the order of precedence for providing alimony to minors, listing parents, adult siblings, grandparents, and other relatives.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CC Article 475">
  <data key="d0">CC Article 475</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">CC Article 475 establishes the order of precedence for providing alimony to adults, listing spouses, descendants, ancestors, and siblings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CC Article 478">
  <data key="d0">CC Article 478</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">CC Article 478 provides an exception to the general order of precedence, stating that if a spouse's subsistence is at risk, the obligation passes to the next debtor in line.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-064be7b670f607c7ad1909fcbe93be8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cónyuge Deudor De Alimentos">
  <data key="d0">Cónyuge Deudor De Alimentos</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cónyuge Deudor De Alimentos is a spouse who has the primary obligation to provide alimony payments, as specified in Article 478 CC.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cónyuge Deudor De Alimentos is a spouse who has the primary obligation to provide alimony payments, as specified in Article 478 CC.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7c041e40623fc8901c57c019e099adf</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783976458</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retensión">
  <data key="d0">Retensión</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Retensión is a legal concept referring to the right of a creditor to retain a debtor's property when the debt is not sufficiently secured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15085c9fb5c051763b8b1a10b6949fa7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973972</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Manumanta Waqaychay">
  <data key="d0">Manumanta Waqaychay</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Manumanta Waqaychay is a legal concept that involves the creditor's right to retain a debtor's property when the debt is not adequately secured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15085c9fb5c051763b8b1a10b6949fa7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973972</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Imperative Form">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Imperative Form</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The imperative form in Quechua is created by adding the suffix 'y' to the verb root, as in 'tiyay' (sit) from the root 'tiya'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Polite Form">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Polite Form</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A polite way of requesting an action in Quechua involves using the suffix 'kuy', such as in 'tiyaikuy' (please sit).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Super-Polite Form">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Super-Polite Form</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An even more polite form in Quechua is achieved by adding the suffix 'puway', as in 'tiyaikapuway' (please, take a seat).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Citizen Empathy">
  <data key="d0">Citizen Empathy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The concept of establishing citizen empathy involves creating an initial connection and understanding with citizens to ensure equal treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Motivational Questions">
  <data key="d0">Motivational Questions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Motivational questions are preliminary inquiries aimed at encouraging citizens to briefly state their issue and identify the appropriate office or official for assistance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Office Location">
  <data key="d0">Office Location</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The concept of office location refers to the physical or virtual space where citizens can receive services or assistance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Greetings">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Greetings</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Quechua greetings vary based on gender and formality, such as 'Wiraqucha haykuyamuy' (Come in, sir) and 'Mamay haykuyamuy Quya haykuyamuy' (Come in, madam).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Imperative Greetings">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Imperative Greetings</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Quechua imperative greetings are used to address multiple people, such as 'Wiraquchakuna haykuykamuy' (Come in, gentlemen) and 'Mamaykuna haykuykamuy Quyakuna haykuykamuy' (Come in, ladies).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Cordial Greetings">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Cordial Greetings</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Quechua greetings can be made more cordial by adding 'kuy' or 'kukuy' to the verb, like 'Tiyaykuy' (Take a seat, please).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Super-Cordial Greetings">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Super-Cordial Greetings</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">For an even more cordial greeting in Quechua, the suffix 'puway' can be added, as in 'Tiyaykukuy' (Please, take a seat).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Silence Request">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Silence Request</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The request for silence in Quechua can be made in singular or plural forms, such as 'Upallasun Ch'inlla' (Be silent) and 'Upallasunchik Ch'inlla' (Be silent, all of you).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Waiting Request">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Waiting Request</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The request for waiting in Quechua can be expressed as 'Suyaykuy, wahayamusunkikuña' (Wait, you will be called) or 'Suyaykuy, waqyamusunkiña' (Wait, you will be called).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Child Safety Request">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Child Safety Request</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A request to ensure child safety on stairs in Quechua is 'Wawayki ama siqanapi puk- llachunchu' (Don't let your child play on the stairs).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Cellphone Request">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Cellphone Request</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The request to turn off cellphones in Quechua can be stated as 'Kamachiwan haykuyta ati- qwaqmi' (Without authorization, you cannot enter) or 'Celularta salaman haykuspa upallachiy' (Turn off your cellphone when entering this room).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Citizen Inquiry">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Citizen Inquiry</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inquiries to understand a citizen's needs in Quechua can be phrased as '¿Imata munanki, mamay? Quya ¿Imatan munanki?' (What do you need, madam?) or '¿Imata munanki, wiraqucha?' (What do you need, sir?).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Citizen Inquiry (Cordial)">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Citizen Inquiry (Cordial)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A more cordial way to inquire about a citizen's needs in Quechua is '¿Imamanni hamuchkanki, mamay? ¿Imamanni hamuchkanki, quya?' (What brings you here, madam?) or '¿Imamanni hamuchkanki, wiraqucha?' (What brings you here, sir?).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Citizen Inquiry (Super-Cordial)">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Citizen Inquiry (Super-Cordial)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An even more cordial way to inquire about a citizen's needs in Quechua is '¿Imapin yanapariykiman?' (How can I help you?) or '¿Imatan qam rayku ruwayman?' (What can I do for you?).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1783973975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quechua Citizen Inquiry (Plural)">
  <data key="d0">Quechua Citizen Inquiry (Plural)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inquiries to understand the needs of multiple citizens in Quechua can be made as '¿Imapin yanapaykikuman?' (How can we help you?) or '¿Yanapariykim?' (Can I help you?).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb8f1cc02966e01b50208ba0de6d31ab</data>
